## Observe Minneapolis

One cell sets the observer, checks the baseline kernel, calls `observe/4`, and
renders the public API result: where each requested body appears on the local
celestial sphere.

```elixir
minneapolis = %{lat: 44.9778, lon: -93.2650, height: 250}
datetime = DateTime.utc_now() |> DateTime.truncate(:second)
bodies = [:sun, :moon, :mercury, :venus, :mars, :jupiter, :saturn]

priv = EphCore.Ephemeris.Kernels.base_dir()
spk = Path.join([priv, "ephemeris", "spk", "de440s.bsp"])

{us, {:ok, obs}} =
  :timer.tc(fn ->
    EphCore.observe(datetime, minneapolis, bodies)
  end)

sky_rows =
  for body <- bodies do
    sky = obs.bodies[body]
    ssp = obs.solar_system_positions[body]
    motion = obs.motion[body]

    %{
      body: body,
      altitude_deg: Float.round(sky.altitude_deg, 2),
      azimuth_deg: Float.round(sky.azimuth_deg, 2),
      ecliptic_lon_deg: Float.round(ssp.ecliptic_longitude, 4),
      lon_rate_deg_day: Float.round(motion.ecliptic_lon_rate_deg_per_day, 4),
      retrograde: motion.retrograde,
      range_km: Float.round(sky.topocentric_range_km, 0)
    }
  end

Kino.render(
  Kino.Markdown.new("""
  **EphCore** `#{Application.spec(:eph_core, :vsn)}` · **#{DateTime.to_iso8601(datetime)}**<br>
  Observer: Minneapolis `#{minneapolis.lat}, #{minneapolis.lon}` (#{minneapolis.height} m)<br>
  SPK `de440s.bsp`: #{if File.exists?(spk), do: "present", else: "missing - run `mix eph.download_kernels`"}<br>
  Runtime: **#{Float.round(us / 1000, 1)} ms**
  """)
)

Kino.DataTable.new(sky_rows, name: "Geometric topocentric sky")
```

## Target Surface

`EphCore.observe/4` currently accepts solar-system bodies from loaded SPK kernels:
Sun, Moon, Mercury, Venus, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune, and Pluto.
Generated asteroid kernels also work when the matching SPK file is installed and
listed in `:spk_kernels`, for example `asteroids/2000001.bsp` for Ceres.
Fixed stars are available through `EphCore.Stars.*` after `mix eph.setup_stars`,
but they do not yet share the `observe/4` target list.

Future releases should let callers pass one mixed list of planets, asteroid
kernels, fixed stars, and later target sources into the same celestial-sphere
API. See [`jakedjohnson/eph_core#1`](https://github.com/jakedjohnson/eph_core/issues/1).

## Frame Choices

A sky engine has to answer different questions with different frames.

```text
timestamp + target bodies
        |
        +--> geocentric state
        |    Earth center, solar-system geometry
        |    useful for orbits, almanac longitudes, stations, ingresses
        |
        +--> topocentric sky
             observer latitude/longitude/height + Earth rotation
             useful for altitude, azimuth, rising/setting, local experience
```

Topocentric calculations matter when the observer's place on Earth matters: the
Moon shifts the most, horizon crossings move by location, and a person traveling
through life does not keep one local sky. Astrology historically leans heavily
on geocentric planetary longitudes, but location-sensitive ideas such as rising
signs, houses, and visible transits require the observer's place on the rotating
Earth. Apparent geocentric calculations matter less as a visible horizon picture
and more when small longitude differences become timing differences, especially
near slow motion or station points. The dependency exposes these choices because
applications need to choose the frame that matches the question.

## Same Instant, Many Observers

A single timestamp can fan out across many observers. In a notebook,
`Task.async_stream/3` is the small version: each location gets an independent
local horizon. In a service, the same idea can become streaming results,
supervised work, cached shared sky state, or per-observer runtime processes.

```elixir
cities = [
  {"Minneapolis", 44.98, -93.27},
  {"Austin", 30.27, -97.74},
  {"London", 51.51, -0.13},
  {"Tokyo", 35.68, 139.69},
  {"Sydney", -33.87, 151.21},
  {"Reykjavik", 64.15, -21.94},
  {"Santiago", -33.45, -70.67},
  {"Nairobi", -1.29, 36.82}
]

{fanout_us, city_rows} =
  :timer.tc(fn ->
    cities
    |> Task.async_stream(
      fn {name, lat, lon} ->
        loc = %{lat: lat, lon: lon, height: 0}
        {:ok, city_obs} = EphCore.observe(datetime, loc, [:sun, :moon, :saturn])

        %{
          city: name,
          sun_alt: Float.round(city_obs.bodies.sun.altitude_deg, 1),
          moon_alt: Float.round(city_obs.bodies.moon.altitude_deg, 1),
          saturn_alt: Float.round(city_obs.bodies.saturn.altitude_deg, 1)
        }
      end,
      ordered: true,
      timeout: 30_000
    )
    |> Enum.map(fn {:ok, row} -> row end)
  end)

Kino.render(
  Kino.Markdown.new(
    "**#{length(cities)} cities × 3 bodies** in #{Float.round(fanout_us / 1000, 1)} ms"
  )
)

Kino.DataTable.new(city_rows, name: "Sun / Moon / Saturn altitude by city")
```

This is the first application-shaped move beyond one snapshot: one verified
calculation pipeline becomes a service primitive for many places, many people,
and eventually many planning questions.

